00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:05.600
The AI analysis continues to learn from volume confirmation patterns, and is adjusting position sizing rules accordingly.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:29.940
Imagine you see the Apple redesign headline at 9.15am and click buy on AAPL in your TFSA. Your order fills at $228.40, yet between the news hit and that fill, the bid asks spread on AAPL widened from 3 cents to 11 cents in the first 12 minutes as high-frequency desks absorb the initial flow.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:41.480
What most retail investors miss is that the first 30 minutes after a headline often show the widest spreads and the highest chance of slippage, especially on names already in the news flow.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:48.680
Professionals always cross-check the 15 minute volume bar against the prior 20 day average before committing size.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.180
If the bar is still below average, they wait for the next 15 minute print.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:09:08.320
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating the headline price as the executable price. Instead, place a limit order 0.3 to 0.5% below the last print and let the market come to you once volume confirms the move.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:20.960
In Canada, the same dynamic appears on TSX names when US headlines spill over, except the currency conversion layer can add another 5 to 10 basis points of effective spread during the first volatile minutes.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:31.820
The key discipline is to treat the first 15 minute bar as a filter rather than an entry signal, preserving the ability to size the position correctly once liquidity normalizes.

